{"subjectId":"java","id":"varargs-overloading","title":"Varargs & Method Overloading","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Overloading = same name, different parameter lists, chosen at <em>compile time</em>. Varargs (<code>T...</code>) lets a method accept zero or more arguments as an array.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Overloading</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int area(int side) { return side * side; }\n    static int area(int w, int h) { return w * h; }\n    static double area(double r) { return Math.PI * r * r; }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(area(3));\n        System.out.println(area(2, 5));\n        System.out.printf(\"%.2f%n\", area(1.0));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>9\n10\n3.14</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Overloads must differ in the <strong>number, types or order</strong> of parameters. Different return types, parameter names, or exceptions alone are not enough.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-dots\"></i>Varargs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int sum(int... nums) {                   // nums is an int[]\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int n : nums) total += n;\n        return total;\n    }\n\n    static String tag(String name, Object... attrs) {   // varargs must be LAST\n        return name + Arrays.toString(attrs);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(sum() + \" \" + sum(1) + \" \" + sum(1, 2, 3));\n        System.out.println(sum(new int[]{4, 5}));       // can pass an array directly\n        System.out.println(tag(\"div\", \"id\", 7, true));\n        System.out.println(String.format(\"%s-%s\", \"a\", \"b\"));   // printf/format use varargs\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 1 6\n9\ndiv[id, 7, true]\na-b</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>Only <strong>one</strong> varargs parameter per method, and it must be the <strong>last</strong> one.</li><li>Inside the method it is an ordinary array (possibly empty, never <code>null</code> unless the caller passes <code>null</code>).</li><li>Each call allocates a new array — hot paths often add fixed-arity overloads (like <code>List.of(e1, e2, …)</code>).</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Overload resolution: which method wins?</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Phase 1 — exact / widening</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Match without boxing or varargs; primitive widening allowed (<code>int</code> → <code>long</code> → <code>float</code> → <code>double</code>).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Phase 2 — boxing / unboxing</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Allow <code>int</code> ↔ <code>Integer</code>, then widening of references (<code>Integer</code> → <code>Number</code> → <code>Object</code>).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Phase 3 — varargs</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Only if nothing matched in phases 1–2.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Most specific</div><div class=\"step-desc\">If several match in the same phase, the most specific wins; if none is more specific → <em>ambiguous</em> compile error.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static void f(long x)    { System.out.println(\"long (widening)\"); }\n    static void f(Integer x) { System.out.println(\"Integer (boxing)\"); }\n    static void f(int... x)  { System.out.println(\"int... (varargs)\"); }\n\n    static void g(Object o)  { System.out.println(\"g(Object)\"); }\n    static void g(String s)  { System.out.println(\"g(String)\"); }\n\n    static void h(double d)  { System.out.println(\"h(double)\"); }\n    static void h(Object o)  { System.out.println(\"h(Object)\"); }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        f(5);              // widening beats boxing beats varargs\n        f(Integer.valueOf(5));\n        f();\n        g(null);           // String is more specific than Object\n        g((Object) \"hi\");  // static (compile-time) type decides\n        h('A');            // char → double widening wins over boxing to Character\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>long (widening)\nInteger (boxing)\nint... (varargs)\ng(String)\ng(Object)\nh(double)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Overloads are chosen from the <strong>compile-time (static) types</strong> of the arguments, not the runtime objects. <code>Object o = \"hi\"; g(o);</code> calls <code>g(Object)</code>. Contrast with overriding, which is chosen at runtime.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static void k(Integer a, long b) {}\n    static void k(long a, Integer b) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        k(1, 2);          // error: reference to k is ambiguous\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>A classic trap: <code>List.remove</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(10, 20, 30, 1));\n        list.remove(1);                       // remove(int index) → removes 20\n        System.out.println(list);\n        list.remove(Integer.valueOf(1));      // remove(Object) → removes the value 1\n        System.out.println(list);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[10, 30, 1]\n[10, 30]</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can we overload main?</summary><div><p>Yes — you can declare other <code>main</code> methods with different parameters, but the JVM only starts the program with <code>public static void main(String[] args)</code> (or <code>String...</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the priority order in overload resolution?</summary><div><p>Exact match / primitive widening first, then boxing/unboxing, then varargs. Within a phase the most specific method is chosen; if none is most specific the call is ambiguous and won't compile.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is void f(int[] a) vs void f(int... a) a valid overload?</summary><div><p>No — varargs compiles to an array parameter, so both have the same signature <code>f(int[])</code>: a <em>duplicate method</em> compile error.</p></div></details></section>"}